## Part Five

Monitoring

#### Article 15

Provisions regarding monitoring as referred to in Article 10 shall apply mutatis mutandis to the implementation of monitoring of the fulfillment of Children's rights and special protection of Children.

##### CHAPTER IV

##### COORDINATION AND COOPERATION

#### Article 16

1. Implementation of coordination and cooperation on Women's and Children's Protection aims to:
   a. improve efforts to fulfill Women's and Children's rights;  

   b. improve synergistic and harmonious working relationships; and  
   c. obtain data and information in the implementation of Women's and Children's Protection.  

2. In order to achieve the objectives as referred to in paragraph (1) the Regional Government cooperates with:
   a. other Regional Governments;  

   b. related agencies;  
   c. non-governmental institutions;  

   d. universities; and  
   e. non-governmental organizations.  
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
   a. exchange of data and information;  
   b. socialization or legal counseling lectures;  
   c. rehabilitation of victims of Violence; and  
   d. repatriation and social reintegration.

# CHAPTER V

RESPONSIBILITY OF THE REGIONAL GOVERNMENT

## Part One

General

#### Article 17

1. The Regional Government is responsible for the protection of Women's and Children's rights.  
2. Responsibility as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
- 11 -
   a. education;  
   b. health;  
   c. politics;  
   d. law;  
   e. economy;  
   f. culture; and  
   g. social.  
3. The Regional Government's responsibility for the protection of Women's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted by:
   a. formulation of gender-responsive Regional policies;  
   b. institutionalization of gender mainstreaming within the Regional Government;  
   c. empowerment of Women in the political, legal, economic, cultural and social fields in community organizations at the regency level;  
   d. strengthening and development of Women's empowerment service provider institutions at the regency level;  
   e. implementing efforts to Prevent the occurrence of Violence against Women.  
4. The Regional Government's responsibility for the protection of Children's rights as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be conducted by:
   a. formulation of policies, programs, and Regional development activities with a child rights perspective that are set forth in Regional development planning documents;  
   b. formulation of norms, standard procedures and assessment criteria for the fulfillment and protection of Children's rights that refer to norms, standards, procedures and criteria established by the government;  
   c. implementation of child-friendly regency shall be carried out in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;  
   d. facilitation of the implementation of Regional Children's forum;  
   e. increasing Community participation in the implementation and protection of Children's rights;  
   f. monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Children's fulfillment and Protection programs;  
   g. guidance of Child Protection institutions;  
   h. providing special protection for Children; and  
   i. implementing efforts to Prevent the occurrence of Violence against Children.
